solnix: zfs -- flush mmap()'d pages before lseek(SEEK_DATA/SEEK_HOLE), so a just-written file is not reported as one
big hole (and cp(1) does not copy it as zeros).

SYMPTOM (kiwi build hosts on OpenIndiana 2026.04 illumos 3581f14f53, AND lixi on solnix ac45d71fc2b0, 2026-10-27):
go 1.27.3 built from source segfaulted, SIGILLed, failed "missing stackmap" / "traceback did not unwind" / ld.so.1 "TLS
requirement failure" -- intermittently, and only for the copy the derivation installs. The installed bin/go had three
128 KiB records (the dataset recordsize) of zeros where the build tree's copy had code and data
(runtime.mallocScanTable 0/81 non-zero; a good build of the same source has 81/81).

MECHANISM (reproduced: holetest.c, 5/5 on the build host, 3/3 on lixi; 0/3 with msync):
- Go's linker writes its output through a MAP_SHARED mapping. Those pages sit in the page cache; zfs_putpage turns
  them into dirty dbufs only when they are pushed (fsync, msync, pageout).
- GNU cp 9.x copies sparse-aware: lseek(SEEK_DATA)/lseek(SEEK_HOLE), then reads only the data extents.
- zfs_holey -> dmu_offset_next -> dmu_object_wait_synced waits for DIRTY DBUFS (illumos 16087). An mmap-written file
  has none yet, so the block pointers still describe holes: the whole file reads as a hole. cp writes zeros. Measured:
  a 32 MiB mmap-written file copied right after close came out 100% different; the same copy 6 s later (txg synced)
  was identical.
- OpenZFS fixed this in zfs_holey_common: "Flush any mmap()'d data to disk" (de198f2d9, 2021), made synchronous in
  102b468b5 (2024, "Fix corruption caused by mmap flushing problems"). illumos never took it.

THE CHANGE (zfs_vnops.c zfs_holey): if the vnode has cached pages, VOP_PUTPAGE(vp, 0, 0, 0 /* sync */) before
dmu_offset_next. The pushed pages become dirty dbufs, and dmu_object_wait_synced then waits for their txg, as it
already does for write(2) data. zfs_fsync uses the same VOP_PUTPAGE call. ZFS_ENTER nests (z_teardown_lock is an
rrm read lock).

diff -ru a/usr/src/uts/common/fs/zfs/zfs_vnops.c b/usr/src/uts/common/fs/zfs/zfs_vnops.c
--- a/usr/src/uts/common/fs/zfs/zfs_vnops.c	2026-10-27 20:01:52.580630826 -0400
+++ b/usr/src/uts/common/fs/zfs/zfs_vnops.c	2026-10-27 20:01:52.600089198 -0400
@@ -282,6 +282,22 @@
 	else
 		hole = B_FALSE;
 
+	/*
+	 * solnix: flush any mmap()'d data first, synchronously (OpenZFS
+	 * de198f2d9 + 102b468b5, zfs_holey_common). Pages written through a
+	 * MAP_SHARED mapping live only in the page cache until putpage turns
+	 * them into dirty dbufs, so dmu_offset_next() -- which waits for dirty
+	 * dbufs to sync -- saw none and reported the whole file as a hole.
+	 * cp(1) and friends then copied zeros (measured: a linker-written Go
+	 * binary lost whole 128K records).
+	 */
+	if (vn_has_cached_data(vp)) {
+		error = VOP_PUTPAGE(vp, (offset_t)0, (size_t)0, 0, kcred,
+		    NULL);
+		if (error != 0)
+			return (error);
+	}
+
 	error = dmu_offset_next(zp->z_zfsvfs->z_os, zp->z_id, hole, &noff);
 
 	if (error == ESRCH)
